WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.093
Take a payment service that can finish 1,000 requests a second.

2
00:00:07.093 --> 00:00:12.740
Its clients send 800, so it's 80 percent busy and answers in about a millisecond.

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.133
Each client waits 200 milliseconds for an answer.

4
00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:20.487
If none comes, it tries again at once, up to four attempts in all.

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.300
For ten seconds, all 800 attempts a second get a useful answer.

6
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.585
The rate of useful answers is called goodput.

7
00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:32.479
At ten seconds, a service it depends on slows down.

8
00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:36.119
For five seconds, it can finish only 400 a second.

9
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:40.232
Now 800 arrive each second and 400 leave, so a queue builds.

10
00:00:40.232 --> 00:00:45.691
Soon a new request waits longer than its client will, and the client sends it again.

11
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:53.288
Every attempt now times out, so each request is sent four times: 3,200 attempts a second.

12
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:58.968
The service answers in order, and each answer goes to a client that gave up long ago.

13
00:00:58.968 --> 00:01:00.392
Goodput falls to zero.

14
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.031
At fifteen seconds the slow service recovers, and capacity is back to 1,000.

15
00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:07.675
It makes no difference.

16
00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:12.787
3,200 attempts a second is more than three times what it can finish.

17
00:01:12.787 --> 00:01:16.477
The queue keeps growing, and goodput stays at zero.

18
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.326
The trigger is gone, but the retries keep the service down.

19
00:01:20.326 --> 00:01:24.137
That's a metastable failure: its own behaviour holds it there.

20
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:26.455
A retry budget breaks the loop.

21
00:01:26.455 --> 00:01:33.093
Retries may add at most 10 percent to the load, so the service never sees more than 880 a second.

22
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:35.435
Run the same slowdown again.

23
00:01:35.435 --> 00:01:43.179
Goodput still drops to zero, but once capacity is back, 880 is less than 1,000, so the queue drains.

24
00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:49.187
Slowly, because the server still works through attempts nobody is waiting for.

25
00:01:49.187 --> 00:01:51.419
At 34 seconds it's back to normal.

26
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:54.820
Retries add load just when a service is slowest.

27
00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.343
A budget keeps a short slowdown short.
